course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 28
title: Shchedryk, New Year, Easter eggs and birthdays
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: You probably know a Ukrainian song already: "Carol of the Bells", the ding-ding-dong Christmas tune, is the Ukrainian song ShchedrYk, a New Year song about a swallow promising a rich year.
explain: Novyi rik, New Year, is a huge family party around the yalYnka, the tree, and everyone shouts "z NOvym rOkom!" — Happy New Year!
explain: In spring comes Velykden, "the Great Day", Easter, with the greeting "KhrystOs voskrEs!" and the fixed answer "VoIstynu voskrEs!".
explain: Easter eggs come in two kinds: pYsanky, works of art drawn with wax and dye, which you admire and never smash, and krAshanky, plain dyed eggs, which kids knock together in "bytky" — the uncracked egg wins.
explain: On birthdays you say "z dnem narodzhennya!" and the family may sing "mnohaya lita", many years; and on 24 August, Den Nezalezhnosti, families wear embroidered shirts and wave the blue-and-yellow flag.
fact: "ShchedrYk" is a Ukrainian New Year song arranged by the composer Mykola Leontovych; its tune is known in English as "Carol of the Bells".
fact: The words of ShchedrYk tell of a swallow flying in to promise the family a rich and happy year.
fact: "Novyi rik" means New Year, a huge family celebration with a tree.
fact: "z NOvym rOkom!" means "Happy New Year!", and the answer is "i vas!" (and to you!) or "z NOvym rOkom!" back.
fact: "yalYnka" means the Christmas or New Year tree.
fact: "Velykden" means Easter, literally "the Great Day"; families take baskets to church, and its date often differs from Easter in England.
fact: "KhrystOs voskrEs!" means "Christ is risen!", the Easter greeting, and the fixed answer is "VoIstynu voskrEs!", "He is risen indeed!".
fact: "pYsanky" are Easter eggs with fine patterns made with wax and dye; they are art and are not used for egg-knocking.
fact: "krAshanky" are plain dyed eggs, and "bytky" is the game of knocking them together: the egg that does not crack wins.
fact: "z dnem narodzhennya!" means "Happy birthday!", and the answer is "DYAkuyu!".
fact: "mnohaya lita", "many years", is a song sung at birthdays and celebrations, instead of or as well as "Happy birthday".
fact: "Den Nezalezhnosti" means Independence Day, on 24 August, marking Ukraine's independence in 1991; families wear vyshyvanky, embroidered shirts, and wave blue-and-yellow flags.
exercise: Which famous English Christmas tune comes from the Ukrainian song ShchedrYk? | Carol of the Bells | Jingle Bells | Silent Night | Deck the Halls
exercise: Which bird flies into the song ShchedrYk? | A swallow | A white stork | A farmyard rooster | A little owl
exercise: Who arranged ShchedrYk? | Mykola Leontovych | Taras Shevchenko | Pyotr Tchaikovsky | Ivan Franko
exercise: What is "Novyi rik"? | New Year | Christmas | Easter Day | A birthday
exercise: At midnight on 31 December everyone shouts what? | z NOvym rOkom! | KhrystOs voskrEs! | z dnem narodzhennya! | DObroi NOchi!
exercise: Sashko's family decorates the "yalYnka". What is it? | The New Year tree | The front door | The birthday cake | The Easter basket
exercise: What does "Velykden" literally mean? | The Great Day | The Holy Night | The Spring Feast | The Egg Day
exercise: On Easter morning Sashko's granny says "KhrystOs voskrEs!". What do you answer? | VoIstynu voskrEs! | SlAvimo Yoho! | z NOvym rOkom! | HeroYam slAva!
exercise: What are "pYsanky"? | Eggs with fine patterns drawn in wax and dye | Plain boiled eggs dyed a single colour in onion skins | Chocolate eggs | Eggs for the knocking game
exercise: What happens in the game "bytky"? | Kids knock dyed eggs and the uncracked one wins | Kids hide eggs in the garden for others to find | Kids roll eggs down a hill | Kids paint eggs in a race
exercise: Which eggs are used for knocking? | krAshanky, plain dyed eggs | pYsanky, patterned art eggs | chocolate eggs | raw eggs
exercise: It's Sashko's birthday. What do you say? | z dnem narodzhennya! | z NOvym rOkom! | SMAchnoho! | DObroi NOchi!
exercise: What is "mnohaya lita"? | A song wishing many years | A birthday cake | A greeting said only at Easter | A New Year game
exercise: When is Den Nezalezhnosti? | 24 August | 1 January | 25 December | 6 December
test: my mate says Carol of the Bells is an old English song. right? | No, its tune comes from the Ukrainian ShchedrYk | Yes, it was written in London for a church choir | No, it is from Germany | Yes, by a church choir in York
test: Your little brother grabs Sashko's granny's pYsanka to play bytky. What's the problem? | pYsanky are art, not for knocking | Nothing, that's what they're for | pYsanky are raw eggs | bytky is played only with chocolate eggs
test: In a game you knock krAshanky: yours cracks, Sashko's doesn't. Who wins? | Sashko | You, the cracked one | Nobody, it's a draw | Whoever's egg is bigger
test: Sashko's granny says "KhrystOs voskrEs!" and you answer "SlAvimo Yoho!". What went wrong? | That's the Christmas answer; Easter needs "VoIstynu voskrEs!" | Nothing, it's right | You should say "DYAkuyu" | You should say "z NOvym rOkom!"
test: Your mate thinks Ukrainian Easter is always on the same day as English Easter. What's true? | The date often differs | It is always the same day | It is always in winter | It is always on 24 August
test: The family sings "mnohaya lita" at grandad's birthday. Why? | To wish him many years | To say goodbye to the guests | To start dinner at the table | To wish everyone a happy Easter
test: Sashko says "z NOvym rOkom!" to you. What's a natural reply? | i vas! | proBAch! | ne lai! | VoIstynu voskrEs!
test: On 24 August Sashko's family wears embroidered shirts and waves flags. What day is it? | Den Nezalezhnosti, Independence Day | Velykden, Easter | Rizdvo, Christmas | Novyi rik, New Year
test: What would happen if you said "z dnem narodzhennya!" on Sashko's birthday? | He'd be pleased and say "DYAkuyu!" | He'd think you said Happy Easter | He'd be offended | He'd ask for his presents back at once
test: In which year did Ukraine become independent, which Den Nezalezhnosti marks? | 1991 | 1945 | 2014 | 1917
exam: Identify the Ukrainian composition whose melody is known in the English-speaking world as "Carol of the Bells". | ShchedrYk | mnohaya lita | kolyadky in general | the national anthem
exam: It is given that the greeting "KhrystOs voskrEs!" has been addressed to a guest on Easter Day. Select the fixed response prescribed by custom. | VoIstynu voskrEs! | SlAvimo Yoho! | HeroYam slAva! | z NOvym rOkom!
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distinction between "pYsanky" and "krAshanky"? | The former are patterned works of art; the latter are plainly dyed eggs used in the knocking game | The former are used in the knocking game; the latter are works of art | Both are chocolate confections | Both are uncooked eggs reserved for baking
exam: Ascertain the date upon which Ukraine observes its Independence Day. | The twenty-fourth of August | The first of January | The twenty-fifth of December | The sixth of December
